## Derivation of the Matrix Representation of the Pullback on a One-Form

### Setup and notation

Let $M$ be an $m$-dimensional manifold with local coordinates $\{x^\mu\}_{\mu=1}^{m}$, and $N$ an $n$-dimensional manifold with local coordinates $\{y^\alpha\}_{\alpha=1}^{n}$. Let $\phi: M \to N$ be a smooth map, expressed in these coordinates as

$$y^\alpha = \phi^\alpha(x^1, \dots, x^m), \qquad \alpha = 1, \dots, n.$$

Let $\omega$ be a one-form on $N$, written in coordinates as

$$\omega = \omega_\alpha(y)\, dy^\alpha \qquad \text{(Einstein summation over } \alpha\text{)}.$$

### Definition of the pullback

The pullback $\phi^*\omega$ is the one-form on $M$ defined pointwise by

$$(\phi^*\omega)_p(v) \;=\; \omega_{\phi(p)}\!\big(d\phi_p(v)\big), \qquad v \in T_p M.$$

Here $d\phi_p : T_p M \to T_{\phi(p)} N$ is the differential (pushforward) of $\phi$ at $p$.

### Pulling back the coordinate one-forms $dy^\alpha$

The key step is to compute $\phi^*(dy^\alpha)$. By definition of the pullback on functions and forms,

$$\phi^*(dy^\alpha) = d(\phi^* y^\alpha) = d(y^\alpha \circ \phi) = d\phi^\alpha.$$

Since $\phi^\alpha$ is a smooth function of the $x^\mu$,

$$d\phi^\alpha = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, dx^\mu \qquad \text{(summation over } \mu\text{)}.$$

Therefore

$$\boxed{\;\phi^*(dy^\alpha) = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, dx^\mu.\;}$$

### Pulling back $\omega$

Using linearity of the pullback and the fact that $\phi^*(f\,\eta) = (\phi^* f)(\phi^*\eta)$ for a function $f$ and form $\eta$:

$$\phi^*\omega = \phi^*\!\big(\omega_\alpha\, dy^\alpha\big) = \big(\omega_\alpha \circ \phi\big)\;\phi^*(dy^\alpha).$$

Substituting the result above,

$$\phi^*\omega = \omega_\alpha\!\big(\phi(x)\big)\;\frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, dx^\mu.$$

Relabeling, the component of $\phi^*\omega$ along $dx^\mu$ is

$$(\phi^*\omega)_\mu = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, \omega_\alpha.$$

### Matrix representation

Write the components of $\omega$ on $N$ as a column vector $\boldsymbol{\omega} = (\omega_1, \dots, \omega_n)^T$ (indexed by $\alpha$), and the components of $\phi^*\omega$ on $M$ as a column vector $(\phi^*\boldsymbol{\omega}) = \big((\phi^*\omega)_1, \dots, (\phi^*\omega)_m\big)^T$ (indexed by $\mu$). Then the relation

$$(\phi^*\omega)_\mu = \sum_{\alpha=1}^{n} \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, \omega_\alpha$$

is the matrix product

$$(\phi^*\boldsymbol{\omega}) = J^T \boldsymbol{\omega},$$

where $J$ is the **Jacobian matrix** of $\phi$,

$$J = \left[\frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\right]_{\substack{\alpha=1,\dots,n \\ \mu=1,\dots,m}}.$$

In index notation, the matrix entries of the pullback operator are precisely the Jacobian entries

$$\boxed{\;(\phi^*)^\alpha_{\;\mu} = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}.\;}$$

### Remark on the index structure

The upper index $\alpha$ ranges over the coordinates of $N$ (the target), and the lower index $\mu$ ranges over the coordinates of $M$ (the source). This is the **same Jacobian matrix** that governs the pushforward of tangent vectors,

$$(\phi_* v)^\alpha = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, v^\mu,$$

reflecting the duality between vectors and one-forms: the pullback on covectors is the **transpose** (dual) of the pushforward on vectors, which is why the same partial-derivative entries appear, with the contraction running over the opposite index. In the problem's notation $\phi_*$, this Jacobian is the matrix representation:

$$(\phi_*)^\alpha_{\;\mu} = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}.$$

### Verification via the defining relation

As a consistency check, apply the pulled-back form to a tangent vector $v = v^\mu \partial_\mu$ at $p \in M$:

$$(\phi^*\omega)_p(v) = (\phi^*\omega)_\mu\, v^\mu = \frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu}\, \omega_\alpha\, v^\mu = \omega_\alpha \left(\frac{\partial y^\alpha}{\partial x^\mu} v^\mu\right) = \omega_\alpha (\phi_* v)^\alpha = \omega_{\phi(p)}(d\phi_p\, v),$$

which is exactly the definition of the pullback. This confirms the matrix representation is correct. $\blacksquare$

